WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:14.400
Big data can be characterized by more than the&nbsp;
three V that we mentioned in the previous session.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.960
Those were volume, velocity, and variety. There&nbsp;
are several practical differences as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.320
Jules Berman has a book called Principles&nbsp;
of Big Data: Preparing, Sharing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:32.400
and Analyzing Complex Information. He lists 10&nbsp;
ways that big data’s different from small data.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:38.880
The first is goals. Small data is&nbsp;
usually gathered for a specific goal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.000
Big data on the other hand may have a&nbsp;
goal in mind when it’s first started, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:52.080
things can evolve or take unexpected directions.
The second is location. Small data is usually in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:58.160
one place, and often in a single computer file.&nbsp;
Big data on the other hand can be in multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.240
files in multiple servers on computers&nbsp;
in different geographic locations.&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.160
Third, the data structure and content. Small&nbsp;
data is usually highly structured like an Excel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:16.320
spreadsheet, and it has rows and columns of data.&nbsp;
Big data on the other hand can be unstructured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.440
it can have many formats in files involved across&nbsp;
disciplines, and may link to other resources.&nbsp;

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:29.840
Fourth, data preparation. Small data is usually&nbsp;
prepared by the end user for their own purposes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:35.040
but with big data the data is often prepared by&nbsp;
one group of people, analyzed by a second group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.600
of people, and then used by a third group of&nbsp;
people, and they may have different purposes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:46.880
and they may have different disciplines.
Fifth, longevity. Small data is usually kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.360
for a specific amount of time after the project&nbsp;
is over because there’s a clear ending point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.800
In the academic world it’s maybe five or&nbsp;
seven years and then you can throw it away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.440
but with big data, each data project,&nbsp;
because it often comes at a great cost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.320
gets continued into others, and so you have data&nbsp;
in perpetuity, and things are going to stay there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:12.720
for a very long time. They may be added on to in&nbsp;
terms of new data at the front, or contextual data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.680
of things that occurred beforehand, or additional&nbsp;
variables, or linking up with different files.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:23.280
So it has a much longer and really uncertain&nbsp;
lifespan compared to a small data set.&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:30.320
The sixth is measurements. Small data is&nbsp;
typically measured with a single protocol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:36.640
using set units and it’s usually done at the same&nbsp;
time. With big data on the other hand, because you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:41.680
can have people in very different places, in&nbsp;
very different times, different organizations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:46.480
and countries, you may be measuring things using&nbsp;
different protocols, and you may have to do a fair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:54.240
amount of conversion to get things consistent.
Number seven is reproducibility. Small data sets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.760
can usually be reproduced in their entirety&nbsp;
if something goes wrong in the process. Big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.560
data sets on the other hand, because they come&nbsp;
in so many forms and from different directions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.840
it may not be possible to start over again if&nbsp;
something’s gone wrong. Usually the best you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.560
can hope to do is to at least identify which&nbsp;
parts of the data project are problematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:21.360
and keep those in mind as you work around them.
Number eight is stakes. On small data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.320
if things go wrong the costs are limited, it’s&nbsp;
not an enormous problem, but with big data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:31.200
projects can cost hundreds of millions of dollars,&nbsp;
and losing the data or corrupting the data can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:36.160
doom the project, possibly even the researcher’s&nbsp;
career or the organization’s existence.&nbsp;

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.880
The ninth is what’s called introspection, and what&nbsp;
this means is that the data describes itself in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:48.320
important way. With small data, the ideal for&nbsp;
instance is what’s called a triple that’s used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:53.200
in several programming languages where you say,&nbsp;
first off, the object that is being measured.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:58.560
In a small data set, things tend to be&nbsp;
well-organized, individual data points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:04.480
can be identified, and it’s usually clear what&nbsp;
things mean. In a big data set however, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.680
things can be so complex with many files and many&nbsp;
formats, you may end up with information that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:16.080
unidentifiable, unlocatable, or meaningless.
The final characteristic is analysis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:22.640
With small data it’s usually possible to analyze&nbsp;
all of the data at once in a single procedure from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:28.400
a single computer file. With big data however,&nbsp;
because things are so enormous and they’re spread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:33.280
across lots of different files and servers, you&nbsp;
may have to go through extraction, reviewing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.760
reduction, normalization, transformation,&nbsp;
and other steps and deal with one part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:42.160
the data at a time to make it more manageable,&nbsp;
and then eventually aggregate your results.&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:44.240
&nbsp;
So it becomes clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:49.360
from this that there’s more than just volume,&nbsp;
and velocity, and variety. There are a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:55.840
of practical issues that can make things more&nbsp;
complex with big data than with small data.

